Ultracentrifugation (UC)
UC is the most extensively used EVs isolation technique and is one of the most commonly used and historically foundational methods. It separates particles based on their differential sedimentation rates under centrifugal force [
9
]. Given the density (1.10–1.19 g/mL) and size differences between EVs, lipoproteins (< 1.10 g/mL), and apoptotic bodies (>200 nm), gradients [
12
] (often made with sucrose or synthetic media) can be used to remove contaminants like cell debris and soluble proteins through differential centrifugation, ultimately enriching EVs [
96
]. The final EVs pellet can be resuspended in PBS or a storage buffer (e.g., Tris-HCl with protease inhibitors) and stored at −80 °C [
97
].
Despite not requiring labeling agents and being applicable to various samples [
98
], UC has significant drawbacks. UC isolates a mixed population defined solely by density and size, rendering it incapable of discriminating EVs subtypes that share identical biophysical properties but originate from distinct cells or possess divergent functions [
10
]. High-intensity centrifugation can damage vesicle structures or cause aggregation. Moreover, UC is inefficient. The standard procedure takes over six hours, with EVs recovery below 50% [
13
]. Its low recovery and propensity to induce aggregation further distort the authentic representation of native EVs heterogeneity [
99
]. While DGC improves purity, it extends the process to over 18 h and drops recovery to under 30%, limiting UC in clinical high throughput testing. However, due to its maturity, UC remains a top choice for proteomic analysis in basic research [
100
].
Ultrafiltration (UF)
UF is a rapid and direct EVs isolation technique, ideal for concentrating EVs pre-isolated by other methods. It leverages the size differences between EVs and other biomolecules (e.g., soluble proteins, lipoproteins) [
101
]. UF membranes have pore sizes of 10–300 kDa (around 3–100 nm), selected based on target EVs size [
102
]. UF membranes with smaller pores preferentially enrich 30–150 nm exosomes, whereas larger-pore membranes are better suited for microvesicles. EVs capture is typically performed via dead-end filtration [
103
](operationally simple but prone to clogging) or tangential-flow filtration (TFF) [
87
](scalable for large volumes with reduced membrane fouling). If required, size-exclusion chromatography can be used for further purification [
104
].
This method offers significant advantages: it is time-efficient (30 min to 2 h), has low equipment costs, and is scalable, making it suitable for preliminary EVs enrichment in clinical samples [
105
]. Its low-shear-force characteristic helps preserve EVs structural integrity, and TFF shows potential in industrial-scale preparation. Moreover, it cannot resolve EVs subpopulations of similar size but distinct biogenesis or cargo, and nonspecific membrane adsorption selectively depletes certain subsets, skewing downstream heterogeneity analyses. In high-viscosity matrices such as plasma, the membrane is prone to clogging and must be replaced frequently [
106
]. To address these issues, researchers have proposed several optimization strategies. For example, combining CME with UF enhances EVs purity to 3.15 times that of UC [
107
]. Another approach involves integrating phospholipid affinity chromatography with UF to efficiently obtain high-purity EVs from urine for metabolomics research [
108
]. Additionally, the co-existence of nanoparticles similar in size to exosomes is another limitation of UF [
109
]. Combining two or more separation methods can overcome this problem [
110
]. For instance, combining TFF with size-exclusion chromatography (TFF-SEC) enables large-scale separation and high-purity preparation simultaneously, offering a reliable solution for standardized production of functional EVs [
111
]. Furthermore, using super-water-absorbent resin (SAP) to concentrate samples reduces the volume for UF processing, indirectly optimizing the efficiency of UF steps. SAP quickly concentrates EVs by absorbing water, with a recovery rate comparable to traditional UF, but at a lower cost and without the need for equipment [
112
].
Method of precipitation
Precipitation-based methods are often used to isolate EVs from complex biological samples, including cell-culture supernatants and organoids, facilitating exploration of their proteomic landscape [
113
]. Being one of the simplest and most widely used EVs isolation techniques due to simple operation and low cost, it is especially suitable for rapid processing of clinical samples [
114
]. The method precipitates EVs from solution by altering their solubility or hydrating layer [
115
]. EVs are induced to aggregate via charge neutralization or hydrophobic interactions [
116
]. Based on this, several commercial kits (e.g., ExoQuick™ and Total Exosome Isolation Kit) enrich EVs in 30 min to 4 h via optimized polymer formulas, making them particularly suitable for low-concentration samples like urine and cerebrospinal fluid.
The advantages of this method are its low equipment requirements, high recovery rate, and high-throughput processing, making it a practical tool for initial EVs screening and large-sample studies [
117
]. However, co-precipitated contaminants like lipoproteins and protein aggregates can reduce EVs purity, and residual polymers may interfere with downstream molecular assays [
116
]. Commercial kits are expensive and not ideal for large samples, and they cannot effectively separate different EV types [
118
]. Issues with batch-to-batch consistency and high costs limit large-scale applications, and precipitation-based methods may not be the best choice for truly descriptive or functional EVs analysis [
119
]. To address these limitations, researchers have optimized PEG precipitation by adjusting PEG concentration, molecular weight, and incubation time. This reduced non-exosomal protein co-precipitation, enhancing exosome purity and yield [
120
]. In another study, optimized PEG precipitation for exosome isolation from MSC culture medium achieved a recovery rate of about 61.74%, comparable to ExoQuick and higher than UC’s 45.80% [
121
]. Additionally, using Ti₃C₂ and Ti₂C materials to interact with EVs for co-precipitation has shown high separation efficiency and purity, effectively removing impurities while maintaining EVs integrity and activity [
122
].
Size-exclusion chromatography (SEC)
SEC separates bio-particles based on differences in hydrodynamic volume. Largely preserving EVs’ native structure and bioactivity, SEC has become a key EVs isolation method [
123
]. It works via the sieving effect of porous media [
124
]. This effectively separates EVs (30–1000 nm) from common contaminants like albumin and lipoproteins [
125
]. Typically, exosomes (30–150 nm) are in the early elution fractions (5–8 mL), with microvesicles and contaminants eluting later [
126
]. When implementing SEC, column selection and buffer optimization are critical [
127
].
SEC is a non-destructive EVs isolation technique that doesn’t damage EVs via mechanical shearing or chemical modification, suitable for downstream functional assays to interrogate functional heterogeneity [
128
]. It removes over 90% of soluble proteins like albumin, boosting EVs purity 5–10 times compared to UC [
129
]. SEC handles sample volumes of 1–100 mL, making it suitable for fine-purifying complex samples like plasma. However, it has some drawbacks. Large elution volumes risk diluting low-abundance yet functionally critical subpopulations [
11
]. The media is prone to lipoprotein clogging, and its limited resolution makes it hard to distinguish between EVs subpopulations of 30–50 nm and 50–100 nm [
130
]. Still, SEC is suggested for quickly and easily obtaining omics-grade urinary-derived EVs in clinical settings, though residual urinary protein pollution needs further optimization [
131
]. Researchers have proposed several improvements. For example, combining the top layer of Sepharose CL-6B with cation-exchange fillers and the bottom layer of multimodal chromatography filler Capto Core 700 reduces co-elution of lipoproteins and albumin, enhancing EVs purity [
132
]. Using a Superose 6 Increase 5/150 column with an FPLC system can separate EVs from 50 µL of plasma within 15 min. The proteomics analysis of repeated samples has a correlation coefficient of over 0.9, meeting the needs of large-scale clinical cohort studies [
133
]. A new device combining hydrophilic porous silica gel (PSG) with a flash liquid chromatography system equipped with a UV detector and fraction collector enables rapid EVs separation [
134
]. Additionally, researchers have transformed SEC into a plate-based method and combined it with custom adapters to automate SEC operations on liquid-handling platforms [
135
]. This optimizes EVs separation with high reproducibility.
Immunoaffinity
Immunoaffinity isolation, a promising EV isolation technique, leverages the specific antigen-antibody interaction to achieve high-purity EVs enrichment by targeting surface markers [
136
,
137
]. The process typically involves: (1) covalently linking specific antibodies (e.g., anti-CD63) to solid supports such as magnetic beads or microbeads; (2) incubating the sample to capture target EVs via the antibodies, followed by washing to remove impurities; and (3) eluting the captured EVs using pH or ionic strength adjustments, or competitive peptides.
This method is celebrated for its high specificity, enabling the direct enrichment of particular EVs subpopulations from complex samples, and is applicable for functional studies and molecular diagnostics [
138
]. Yet its limitations also stem from heterogeneity itself: the high cost of antibodies, the restriction of capture efficiency by antigen expression heterogeneity [
139
], and the susceptibility of EVs bioactivity to pH and salt concentrations [
100
]. The elution process may compromise EVs functionality by disrupting surface membrane proteins [
140
]. To tackle these issues, researchers have developed the GlyExo-Capture platform. It uses affinity-capture magnetic beads to specifically bind particular exosome glycans, completing glycosylated EVs extraction in just 11 min and enhancing miRNA detection sensitivity and specificity [
141
]. In another study, researchers immobilized Tim4 on the surface of Fe₃O₄@SiO₂-ILI-01 magnetic nanomaterials to capture PS on the EVs membrane surface. Results indicated that this method achieved an EVs recovery rate of over 90%, with a purity 7.5 times higher than that of the UC method [
142
].
Anion exchange chromatography (AEC)
AEC separates EVs based on charge-interaction. It uses the binding of negatively charged EVs groups (e.g., sialic acid, phosphate groups) to positively charged ligands in the column. In EVs separation, AEC shows unique advantages in large-scale preparation and subpopulation sorting [
143
]. In AEC, quaternary-ammonium-based anion-exchange resins capture EVs via electrostatic interaction, allowing neutral or positively charged particles (such as some lipoproteins) to pass through [
144
]. Resin selection tailored to EVs heterogeneity is therefore pivotal.Strong-anion-exchange resins (e.g., Q Sepharose FF) are good for large sample processing. Weak-anion-exchange resins (e.g., DEAE Sepharose) can reduce EVs damage. High-capacity resins (e.g., Capto Q) are suitable for viscous samples (e.g., cerebrospinal fluid).
AEC is highly valued in the EVs purification field due to its cost-effectiveness, ease of operation, high efficiency, and scalability. It introduces the critical dimension of charge-based separation in addition to particle size-based methods [
145
], meeting the needs of clinical laboratories for processing large-volume EVs-rich culture media [
146
]. In large-scale preparation, it can process over 100 mL of sample per run, with a recovery efficiency exceeding 90%, and effectively separates lipoproteins, meeting clinical-grade production standards [
147
]. Through gentle electrostatic interactions, it preserves EVs bioactivity and significantly reduces protein contamination compared to UC. Studies have confirmed that AEC can efficiently and massively isolate high-purity EXOs and MVs, revealing differences in protein composition, bioactivity, and charge properties, thus providing a solid foundation for subsequent research and clinical applications [
148
]. However, it has limitations. The desalting step after high-salt elution may introduce residual risks [
148
]. Its resolution is limited, making it hard to distinguish EVs subpopulations with similar charges [
145
]. Some vesicle subtypes show changes in size distribution and purity during elution, indicating the need for multi-parameter analysis to ensure separation purity. Combining high-performance anion-exchange chromatography (HPAEC) with TFF can enhance bEVs purity [
144
,
149
]. Also, cation-exchange chromatography is applicable in specific scenarios, such as separating platelet-derived EVs, due to the presence of single cationic domains on EVs surfaces [
150
].
Innovative isolation methods
Emerging technologies are pushing the resolving power of EVs subpopulation separation beyond bulk isolation, enabling precision sorting based on increasingly refined parameters and offering unprecedented insight into EVs heterogeneity
