Asymmetric flow field-flow fractionation (AF4)
AF4 is a label-free, hydrodynamic technique that resolves EVs by size, enabling high-resolution sorting from complex biological samples and direct visualization of EVs population heterogeneity [
154
]. Its core principle involves the synergistic action of two mobile phases: a vertical cross-flow and a horizontal channel flow [
155
]. AF4 has unique advantages in separating nanoscale biological particles and has been successfully applied in the characterization of viruses, protein complexes, and synthetic nanoparticles, a powerful tool for characterizing EV heterogeneity in complex specimens [
156
]. Its main features are: (1) high-resolution separation capability to distinguish vesicle subpopulations with size differences as small as 10 nm; (2) label-free property avoiding interference from antibodies or foreign polymers, thus preserving the natural state of samples [
157
]; and (3) good compatibility with complex biological samples, usually requiring only minimal pretreatment for analysis. However, AF4 has limitations such as high instrument costs, limited single-batch processing volume (typically < 500 µL), and complex operational parameters. Systematic optimization of key conditions like cross-flow gradients and membrane material selection is needed [
158
].
To address the technical challenges of AF4, researchers have implemented several improvements. Optimizing cross-flow gradients, focusing time, and sample pretreatment conditions can significantly reduce lipoprotein co-isolation in plasma samples while enhancing vesicle integrity and experimental reproducibility [
159
]. One study combined AF4 with DGC to improve EVs purity and reduce non-EV interference [
160
]. Another integrated UF-SEC with AF4 and UV-MALS detection, establishing an efficient workflow for urine vesicle purification and characterization that provides size distribution and concentration data [
161
]. Additionally, coupling AF4 with large-volume sample stacking and capillary electrophoresis (LVSS/CE) enables single-step separation of small serum proteins and non-vesicular matrix components, yielding high-purity EVs suitable for downstream protein analysis [
162
].
Microfluidics
Microfluidics integrates multimodal separation principles into a single device to perform multiparametric sorting that confronts the complexity of EVs heterogeneity. Their high-speed, high-purity operation enables rapid isolation of specific subpopulations from minute clinical specimens, markedly accelerating heterogeneity-based biomarker discovery. For example, the EXODUS system uses pressure fluctuations and dual-frequency vibrations for automated, label-free exosome purification from biological samples, outperforming traditional methods in separation speed, product purity, and recovery rate [
151
]. Another team’s magnetic bead-based droplet microfluidic platform, leveraging immunaffinity, captures vesicles efficiently, achieving 2.5 times higher capture efficiency in shorter incubation times, with a 2 mL sample capacity and over 400 µL/h processing throughput [
163
]. A microchip-based platform enables personalized nanodrug delivery by capturing, enriching, loading, and eluting patient-derived tumor EVs (PT-EVs), completing the entire process within 12 h at a 65% recovery rate, surpassing ultracentrifugation [
164
]. Utilizing the flow-path-to-circuit analogy design strategy, researchers developed a microfluidic pulsatile filtration system. By cascading a 600-nm pore size filter membrane with a 20-nm pore size filter membrane, EVs can be efficiently and completely separated and purified from whole blood samples within 30 min, with a recovery rate as high as 80% [
165
].
To meet clinical sample requirements, several optimization solutions have been proposed. The FLOAT technique, grounded in acoustofluidics, efficiently isolates EVs within minutes, achieving over 90% recovery. Its ability to distinguish uEVs from high-concentration Tamm-Horsfall protein is crucial for surmounting hurdles in EVs-based liquid biopsy development [
166
]. The ExoCAS-2 system uses poly-cation-modified magnetic beads to obtain high-purity vesicles from plasma within 40 min, showing good reproducibility and scalability [
167
]. The ExoCPR platform combines bubble-driven mixing and surface-tension filtration for automated vesicle separation and gentle recovery in just 30 min [
168
]. Additionally, a micro-nano fluidic device combining size screening and antibody-based affinity capture has specifically enriched and analyzed liposarcoma-derived vesicles [
169
]. Based on a viscoelastic microfluidic platform, EVs can be isolated from human blood in a label-free manner. The results show that the purity and recovery rate exceed 97% and 87%, respectively. Notably, compared with the gold standard UC, this method also provides a significant increase in EVs yield [
170
].
For standardization and large-scale applications, the novel EVics system combines TFF and light scattering detection for simultaneous vesicle separation and quantification. Supporting sample volumes from 15 µL to 2 L, it offers superior separation efficiency and purity for 50-to 200-nm vesicles compared to existing technologies [
171
]. These technological breakthroughs not only enhance separation performance but also provide a foundation for high-throughput clinical sample analysis and precision medicine research.
Magnetic bead separation technology
Magnetic bead-based separation captures target vesicles via specific molecular recognition and uses a magnetic field for efficient isolation. It is highly selective, easy to perform, and rapid, making it ideal for precise sorting of specific vesicle subtypes. It empowers researchers to isolate specific subpopulations tailored to the biological question at hand. However, challenges like residual magnetic beads and high costs remain, necessitating stringent quality control in subsequent analyses.
In recent years, a series of innovative magnetic bead-based separation platforms have emerged. For instance, the MESS2CAN strategy uses novel lipid probes and streptavidin-modified magnetic beads. It achieves a 49.6% EVs recovery rate within 1 h and, combined with Cas12a-PER dual signal amplification, can detect target proteins at a sensitivity of 10 EV particles per microliter [
172
]. MagExo technology enhances separation efficiency by optimizing adsorption conditions between magnetic microparticles and vesicles, achieving double the efficiency of traditional ultracentrifugation while maintaining high purity [
173
]. Additionally, a PS-MIPs and NELISA-based Surface-Enhanced Raman Scattering (SERS) detection platform has been developed. This MIPs-NELISA-SERS technology enables non-invasive, high sensitivity, and rapid detection of target substances [
152
]. Another study developed BiMBs with Ti (IV) and DSPE modification. Their synergistic action improves affinity for EVs surface phospholipids and phosphorylated proteins. This results in over 80% recovery within 1 h, and the CD9 signal detected is 2.7 times stronger than that from UC [
174
]. Chitosan oligosaccharide lactate conjugated 1-pyrenecarboxylic acid (COL-Py) based self-assembled magnetic nanoclusters (CMNCs) can target the lipid bilayer of EVs, enabling efficient EVs separation under a magnetic field. Analyses show that EVs extracted via this method have a purity superior to that achieved by UC, with good structural integrity [
175
].
Lipid-nanoprobe-based separation technology
Lipid-nanoprobe-based separation tech captures and releases EVs efficiently by mimicking natural membrane interactions. It uses lipid-molecule binding with vesicle membranes, combined with photo- or enzyme- induced cleavage, to simplify workflows while keeping EVs structurally intact. Its key benefits include a short separation time (< 4 h), high-purity output, and no need for ultracentrifugation equipment, thus offering a feasible option for clinical translation.
Innovative applications have emerged in lipid-nanoprobe-based EVs separation. Photo-lipid probes can release near-native EVs in an hour via light-triggered cleavage, improving separation efficiency and purity [
153
]. The EV-FISHER platform optimizes this approach with cleavable lipid probes (PSDC) and conventional centrifugation, reducing separation time to 40–240 min. It outperforms traditional ultracentrifugation in isolation efficiency (74.2%−18.1%) and requirements (12800–135000 g) [
176
]. The MESSAGE strategy meets clinical diagnostic needs via a novel approach combining lectin-mediated EVs aggregation and smart-algorithm analysis. It can classify early-stage cancer and monitor metastasis using only 20 µL of plasma within two hours, showing high sensitivity and specificity [
177
].
Novel composite technologies are driving the field toward multifunctionality. A strategy integrating proximity ligation assays and DNA-RNA hybridization precisely identifies specific EVs subpopulations with dual-membrane-protein markers. By capturing EVs with magnetic beads and releasing them via enzymatic digestion, this method achieves high-purity EVs separation. It supports high-throughput sequencing and offers a new biomarker-screening platform for breast cancer diagnosis, showing great potential [
178
].
Solid-phase medium separation technology
Separation tech based on functionalized solid-phase media enables efficient EVs capture and specific recognition by tailoring material surface properties. Using chemical modification or biomolecule-coupling strategies, it allows directional binding between solid-phase materials and specific vesicle-surface components (e.g., lipids, proteins, or glycans). This approach offers high selectivity and operational flexibility, providing new ideas for separating and analyzing EVs in complex biological samples.
Typical technologies involve the development of lipid-anchored materials. Take DSPE-functionalized agarose solid-phase medium (DSPE-Beads) for example. By modifying the surface of the solid-phase medium with hydrophobic molecules, it can directly insert into the lipid bilayer of vesicles to capture them rapidly [
179
]. This method boasts high separation efficiency, compatibility with subsequent proteomic analysis, fine stability, and extensive target coverage. To further enhance it, researchers designed porous materials (SOM-ZIF-8-DSPE) that match the size of vesicles. This increases capture capacity and mass transfer efficiency while reducing structural interference [
180
]. Considering the complexity of clinical samples, new phase-transition separation technologies have emerged. By utilizing phase-separated zwitterionic (ZW) aggregates, this method can stably work in body fluids with different pH and ionic strengths. Its anti-fouling properties combined with affinity probe modification enable high-purity enrichment and damage-free release of vesicles in urine samples [
181
]. Additionally, DNA-nanostructure-based intelligent recognition platforms show unique advantages. By adjusting the 3D structure and antibody labeling strategy, this technology can screen vesicle subpopulations with specific sizes and surface markers. It offers a high-precision tool for studying heterogeneity and developing disease biomarkers [
182
].
Membrane curvature-sensing peptides
The membrane of EVs exhibits pronounced geometric curvature; the outer leaflet is enriched in anionic phospholipids and a high proportion of unsaturated lipids, generating characteristic lipid-packing defects [
183
,
184
]. Within the framework of EVs population heterogeneity, this topological signature establishes a structural basis for curvature-sensing peptides; via curvature-dependent interactions, these peptides act as integrated ligands to enable high-efficiency EVs capture and downstream analysis [
185
]. Curvature-recognition mechanisms not only expand the theoretical arsenal for EVs sorting but also enable precise isolation at the subpopulation level: peptides that preferentially bind highly curved membrane domains can selectively enrich EVs subtypes with pronounced curvature differences [
186
]. Compared with conventional protein ligands, curvature-sensing peptides offer superior chemical stability, providing a robust foundation for their deeper integration into EVs isolation workflows [
186
]. For instance, a membrane-sensing peptide (MSP) integrated into a single-molecule array (SiMoA) platform acts as a universal affinity probe, eliminating the selectivity bias inherent to antibody-based methods and enabling label-free, high-sensitivity profiling of all small EVs irrespective of their protein markers [
187
]. In another study, the curvature-sensing peptide NIC4 exploits pH-tunable electrostatic-hydrophobic cooperativity to achieve high-efficiency, high-purity capture and release of EVs, facilitating rapid exosome isolation and in situ single-particle visualization without reliance on canonical exosomal markers [
188
]. ApoC-TRC₁₂, a curvature-targeted far-red probe created by conjugating the curvature-sensing amphipathic helix ApoC to the hydrophobic fluorophore TR-C₁₂, enables centrifugation-free, antibody-free “mix-and-read” detection of EVs irrespective of their protein-marker profile [
189
].
Comparison and evaluation
In the research journey of EVs, the advancement of separation techniques has been a key driving force for the development of this field. The choice of EVs separation techniques needs to consider a variety of factors comprehensively, including sample type, target EVs subpopulations, downstream applications, and equipment accessibility
(
