In recent years, EVs separation tech has evolved from traditional physical methods towards higher specificity, throughput, and clinical applicability. While conventional methods like UC and SEC are well-established, they struggle to balance purity and efficiency [
8
,
190
]. In comparison, emerging technologies show significant advantages: the microfluidic platform EXODUS achieves label-free exosome purification from plasma within 30 min, with a purity 1.6 times higher than UC [
151
]. Magnetic nanomaterials based on Tim4 protein (Fe3O4@SiO2-ILI-01), targeting phosphatidylserine (PS), increase recovery to over 90% and support non-invasive monitoring of PD-L1-positive exosomes [
142
].
Technology integration has become key to overcoming sample-processing bottlenecks. When selecting technology combinations, it’s essential to consider sample characteristics and research goals. In the isolation of uEVs, traditional UC is easily affected by high-abundance urinary protein UMOD. Researchers combined 0.22 μm membrane filtration with UC (F + UC) and a TFE digestion system. This F + UC approach physically blocks UMOD formed fiber networks, achieving over 95% removal, while TFE digestion improves transmembrane protein detection by about 40% compared to traditional urea treatment [
191
]. For EVs isolation from solid tissues, conventional methods often lead to cell debris contamination. A team addressed this by combining mild enzymatic digestion with differential centrifugation and iodixanol DGC. This reduced intracellular marker contamination and revealed the unique protein distribution of tissue-derived small EVs [
192
]. To meet large-scale production needs, researchers proposed using TFF for EVs concentration and buffer exchange, followed by SEC with Cytiva™ superSEC fillers. This reduced purification cycle time from 150 to 45 min while meeting GMP standards [
13
]. To boost EVs yield, researchers have engineered chemical enhancers-such as NA-TPP or NA-photosensitizer conjugates-that, in a single co-culture step, suppress bacterial peptidoglycan synthesis and trigger outer-membrane protrusion, increasing outer-membrane vesicle (OMV) production by 7–65-fold [
193
]. The approach is operationally simple, obviates genetic manipulation, and simultaneously enables functional drug loading across diverse Gram-negative species.These innovative integrated solutions, combining physical separation, chemical treatment, and chromatographic purification, enhance EVs purity, recovery, and functional research reliability, providing robust experimental support for EVs-related studies.
Future development directions for EVs separation technology primarily focus on three aspects. First, there is an urgent need to establish standardized protocols as current EVs isolation platforms show significant differences in EVs size distribution and marker expression [
151
,
194
]. Second, scalable production faces many challenges. Although TFF and microfluidic technologies have increased throughput to some extent, the high shear stress generated by TFF may damage EVs, while microfluidic devices are expensive and complicated to operate. Therefore, there is an urgent need to develop scalable hybrid processes (e.g., TFF-SEC) to meet the growing clinical demands. Third, function-oriented separation technologies need further improvement. By targeting specific markers of EVs subpopulations, it is possible to design more targeted isolation strategies. As technology continues to advance, the application prospects of EVs separation technologies in disease diagnosis, prognosis evaluation, and therapy are becoming increasingly broad. Given this, it is essential to introduce next-generation methods to replace current EVs purification technologies and ensure a significant increase in EVs yield [
119
]. The development of faster, more sensitive, and specific EVs isolation technologies is of great significance for promoting the in-depth development of EVs research and will also provide more robust support for clinical applications [
195
].
Current state of extracellular vesicle detection technologies
Current studies indicate that most published EVs samples are affected by heterogeneous subpopulations and possible non-vesicular contaminants, challenging the reliability of downstream omics analyses [
196
]. Thus, establishing a quality evaluation system with key parameters (particle size, concentration, surface charge, and purity) is now a crucial prerequisite for EVs research [
197
].
Biophysical property detection of extracellular vesicles
Methods for detecting the physical properties of EVs can determine their key physical characteristics, such as size, shape, and surface charge
(
Fig.
5
)
. Physical characterization provides the first layer of insight into heterogeneity by revealing differences in size, morphology, and mechanical properties—features that closely mirror vesicle biogenesis pathways and cellular origin [
198
].
Fig. 5
Physical Characterization of Extracellular Vesicles. The physical characteristics of EVs, including size, shape, and mechanical properties, are crucial for understanding their biological functions. These can be analyzed using techniques such as TEM, SEM, Cryo-EM, AFM, DLS, NTA and TRPS, which together provide a comprehensive toolkit for studying EVs’ physical properties
Microscopy
In the field of EVs morphology characterization, Transmission electron microscopy (TEM), Scanning electron microscopy (SEM), cryo-electron microscopy (Cryo-EM), and atomic force microscopy (AFM) each have unique features. Together, they form a comprehensive technical system for studying EVs ultrastructure.
TEM is the most extensively used tool for assessing EVs sample purity verification and morphological preliminary screening, as it distinguishes individual EV from non-EV particles of similar size [
199
]. As a classic technique, TEM uses a high-energy electron beam to penetrate samples, resolving intricate structures down to 0.1 nm [
200
]. Its advantages lie in the relatively simplified operation process. When combined with negative staining, it can clearly present the typical cup-shaped morphology [
12
]. Moreover, specific labeling techniques, such as immunogold labeling, can accurately locate surface markers, thereby enhancing detection specificity [
200
,
201
]. However, chemical fixation in traditional sample preparation may cause vesicle structural collapse, limiting the interpretation of the native state.
SEM creates images via surface electron signals, offering a 3D view of vesicle morphology with a resolution of 3–10 nm [
202
]. This makes it particularly useful for analyzing surface texture and distribution patterns [
203
]. Unlike the 2D projections of TEM, SEM provides more intuitive spatial imaging, allowing for detailed observation of EVs distribution and aggregation over larger areas [
204
]. However, sample preparation for SEM involves complex steps like dehydration and metal coating [
205
], which may introduce artifacts or damage vesicle integrity.
Recently, Cryo-EM has advanced rapidly, visualizing a wide range of EVs with different sizes, morphologies, lipid bilayers, and internal structures [
206
]. Unlike traditional TEM sample preparation that collapses EVs structures into cup-shaped forms, Cryo-EM preserves membrane shapes via cryogenic techniques [
207
]. Rapid freezing maintains EVs in their native hydrated state, avoiding damage from chemical fixation and staining. It also enables single particle analysis for determining 3D membrane protein structures at 0.3 nm resolution [
208
]. Moreover, improved overall resolution reaches atomic or near-atomic levels, confirming lipid bilayer existence and offering new insights into EVs biogenesis and function [
209
]. The emerging DNA affinity lattice technology overcomes limitations of traditional holey-carbon cryo-EM grids, such as low vesicle heterogeneity and concentration. By using DNA-based affinity lattices on porous carbon cryo-EM grids, it enables efficient enrichment and separation of low-concentration vesicles [
208
]. Despite its great potential in structural biology, Cryo-EM’s application is currently limited by high equipment and sample purity requirements.
AFM is a potent multifunctional tool that non-destructively characterizes the structure and mechanics of biological systems in aqueous conditions with unmatched spatiotemporal resolution [
210
]. Using nano-probe scanning, it can obtain 3D morphology of vesicles with a vertical resolution of 0.1 nm, measure EVs’ real-height, and acquire mechanical properties via force spectroscopy [
211
]. These features provide dynamic parameters for studying vesicle-cell interactions [
212
]. However, AFM imaging is slow, especially for large-area samples, and is more suitable for small-size samples. The imaging efficiency and quality may be affected for larger EVs samples.
Each microscopy technique has limitations: TEM and SEM sample preparation can introduce artifacts; Cryo-EM requires high-purity samples and advanced equipment; AFM is limited by detection efficiency. Multi-technique integration is an optimization strategy. For example, combining TEM and AFM can obtain morphological and mechanical data simultaneously, while combining Cryo-EM and SEM can comprehensively analyze the internal and external structures of vesicles. With the development of techniques like Cryo-EM tomography and high-speed AFM, it is expected that the dynamic relationship between vesicle structure and function will be revealed at higher spatiotemporal resolution in the future.
Advances in super-resolution microscopy (SRM) now allow high-resolution imaging of EVs by circumventing the diffraction limit through single-molecule localization or structured illumination. These techniques provide nanometric details of EV morphology, size, surface-marker distribution, and molecular composition, enabling precise discrimination of EVs subtypes [
213
]. Using STORM, researchers resolved the ring-like architecture of CD81 on individual EVs and performed in situ, multiparametric profiling of single-cell-secreted vesicles-simultaneously quantifying diameter, HLA-DR intensity, and secretion frequency-thereby uncovering striking inter-subtype differences in EVs output among macrophage populations [
214
]. This work underscores the unique capacity of SRM to dissect single-cell EV heterogeneity and functional phenotypes.
Dynamic light scattering (DLS)
DLS, a non-destructive analytical technique based on light scattering, measures nanoparticle sizes by detecting Brownian motion in solution. It’s widely used for assessing EVs size distribution and dispersion homogeneity and provides data on other biophysical properties [
215
,
216
], such as polydispersity, aggregation, solubility, viscosity, and stability [
217
]. The core principle is that when a laser illuminates the sample, the random movement of particles causes the scattered light intensity to fluctuate over time. Mathematical analysis of these fluctuations can calculate the hydrodynamic diameter of the particles [
218
].
DLS is a convenient tool for quickly analyzing EVs size distribution and is suitable for the preliminary assessment of EVs sample quality, such as purity and homogeneity [
19
]. Its non-invasive sample preparation, which requires no chemical fixation or marking, allows for complete sample recovery. The simplicity of the operation also makes DLS user-friendly and accurate. In addition, DLS can be used to measure the zeta potential of EVs. With these advantages, DLS has become a suitable device for determining the routine use of EVs size distribution. However, DLS cannot define the concentration of vesicles, nor can it provide biochemical data or information on EVs sources [
219
]. It usually only provides information on particle size distribution and cannot directly determine parameters such as particle shape or structure. For high-concentration samples, multiple scattering effects may occur, affecting the accuracy of the results [
220
].
Nanoparticle tracking analysis (NTA)
NTA, analyzing EVs via single particle visualization and dynamic tracking, can simultaneously detect size distribution, concentration, and surface characteristics, making it a key method for vesicle quantification [
221
]. It works through laser scattering and Brownian motion: when a laser illuminates the sample, vesicle-scattered light is captured by a microscope. Single particle trajectory tracking calculates diffusion rates, and mathematical models then determine particle sizes [
222
].
NTA is a central quantitative technology in EVs research. It’s highly sensitive and high-throughput, able to detect low-concentration EVs quickly and suit large-scale sample analysis [
223
]. Measuring both size and concentration helps assess EVs heterogeneity. Also, it’s relatively easy to operate with highly automated data collection and analysis [
224
]. However, NTA is very particular about sample conditions like dilution, EVs purity, and temperature, which can greatly affect results [
225
]. Instrument settings, video recording, and data analysis also need calibration and optimization for consistent outcomes [
226
]. Note that NTA’s detection range (30–1000 nm) may miss very small or large vesicles. Compared to DLS, which can be biased by large-particle signals, NTA’s size measurements are closer to the true values [
227
].
Tunable resistive pulse sensing (TRPS)
TRPS, a nanopore-based single-particle analysis technique, measures EV size, concentration, and surface charge with high precision by detecting resistance changes as particles pass through a pore [
228
]. Its core principle is that when a particle traverses a nanopore filled with electrolyte, it causes a transient change in pore resistance, generating an electrical pulse. The pulse intensity correlates with particle volume, and the pulse duration reflects the particle’s transit speed. From this, vesicle size and count can be simultaneously calculated [
229
].
TRPS is crucial for high-precision EVs characterization, especially in clinical research. It offers multi-parameter quantitative analysis of individual EV, providing accurate size distribution and concentration data for complex biological samples [
230
]. Additionally, by measuring surface charge and using zeta potential analysis, TRPS helps explore EV-cell interactions [
231
]. It features a fast measurement speed, simple workflow, and low sample consumption, making it suitable for high-throughput analysis [
232
]. However, its detection range is limited to 40 nm–10 μm, and nanoparticle quantification can be affected by ionic current fluctuations [
233
]. Regular calibration with standard particles before each experiment is necessary to ensure nanopore performance. Also, avoid repeated sample freeze-thaw cycles and ensure thorough mixing before detection to prevent particle aggregation or excess [
97
].
Biochemical characterization of extracellular vesicles
Although biophysical methods are indispensable for quantifying EVs population diversity, they fall short of exposing the molecular determinants of functional heterogeneity. Biochemical analyses penetrate this molecular core, identifying and quantifying the proteins, nucleic acids, and lipids that endow distinct EVs subtypes with specific functionalities. These biochemical analyses comprehensively support the discovery of vesicle biomarkers and their functions, enhance the understanding of EVs roles in cellular communication and disease processes, and boost their applications in biomarker discovery and therapeutic development
(
Fig.
6
)
.
Fig. 6
Detection and Identification of Chemical Properties of Extracellular Vesicles. EVs contain key biochemical components like proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, and metabolites. To support biomarker discovery and functional research, various techniques are used, including WB, ELISA, PCR, SPR, MS, FCM, SERS, and TIRF. These tools provide rich data for studying EVs’ composition and function
Western blot
Western blotting, a standard method for validating vesicle-specific protein markers, precisely detects target protein expression and molecular weight via antigen-antibody interactions [
203
]. In Western Blot identification of EVs, multiple markers are usually detected to ensure the accuracy and reliability of the identification results [
234
]. Extensive research indicates that EVs proteins typically include membrane, cytoplasmic, and nuclear proteins [
98
]. According to the MISEV2023 guidelines released by the International Society for Extracellular Vesicle Research (ISEV) [
235
], WB identification of EVs should analyze at least one protein from transmembrane proteins, cytoplasmic proteins and non-EV co-isolated structures (NVEPs), which act as EVs markers and purity controls.
ELISA
ELISA, operating on specific antigen-antibody interactions, precisely quantifies specific protein markers on/in EVs, making it key for disease biomarker screening and functional studies [
155
]. Compared to Western Blot, ELISA is more convenient, sensitive, and capable of high-throughput analysis [
103
]. It can be combined with other technologies, such as high-throughput droplet digital ELISA, which can produce 20 million droplets per minute for enhanced sensitivity and specificity [
236
]. While both ELISA and Western blotting have similar detection limits, ELISA is generally faster and more scalable for high-throughput measurements [
237
]. However, ELISA has limitations in EVs detection, like the need for high-quality specific antibodies and certain EVs purity requirements.
Mass spectrometry (MS)
Mass spectrometry is the cornerstone for unbiased discovery and comprehensive dissection of EVs molecular heterogeneity. Proteomic and lipidomic profiling can reveal striking molecular disparities among samples or isolated fractions, thereby linking physical subpopulations to specific molecular signatures and furnishing a mechanistic basis for functional heterogeneity [
238
]. LC-MS and MALDI-MS are widely used to characterize EVs proteins [
239
], lipids [
240
], and metabolites. After purification and digestion, EVs proteins can be analyzed by LC-MS in data-dependent or all-ion modes, identifying thousands of proteins. Combining isotopic labeling or label-free quantification allows precise quantification of target molecules. Recent advancements include workflows like Exo-PLA coupled with HRMS for discovering and validating novel EVs surface proteins [
241
]. CDMS analyzes particle charge-to-mass ratios, revealing heterogeneity in mEVs [
242
]. iEVXL elucidates key vesicle-signaling mechanisms by determining protein-complex conformations [
243
].
MS, with high sensitivity, resolution, and coverage, offers extensive proteomic data, aiding in understanding EVs biology and disease mechanisms [
244
]. However, it requires specialized equipment, skilled technicians, and has complex workflows with high requirements for EVs purity and protein extraction efficiency [
245
]. Despite challenges in analyzing low-abundance substances and complex data interpretation, the future development of single-EV MS imaging and multi-omics data integration is expected to enhance the application value of EVs in disease biomarker discovery and mechanistic research [
246
]. Systematic MS analysis can reveal EVs heterogeneity and provide a molecular basis for developing EV-based liquid biopsies and targeted therapies [
247
].
Flow cytometry (FCM)
FCM, with single-particle analysis and multi-parameter detection capabilities, is widely used in EVs phenotyping and subgroup sorting. However, EVs are small, heterogeneous, and hard to measure. FCM faces challenges in EVs and submicron particle analysis, with controversial results often due to instrument limitations, lack of reliable methods, and data-interpretation ambiguities [
248
]. The statistical description of EVs size distribution measured by FCM can be biased by the lower limit of detection (LoD). Thus, defining the lower LoD of flow cytometers is crucial when reporting EVs diameters, and caution is needed in using statistics to describe size distribution [
249
].
Through technological improvements, such as enhanced side scatter detection and optimized fluorescent labeling strategies, or the use of nanoflow cytometry systems (e.g., Apogee, CytoFLEX Nano), precise identification of vesicles in the 100–200 nm range can be achieved [
250
].The advent of nano-flow cytometry (nFCM) has been transformative, enabling simultaneous interrogation of individual EVs for both physical parameters and surface-protein markers, thereby providing direct and quantitative evidence of extensive heterogeneity in surface-molecule expression across the EV population. Through nFCM, it’s confirmed that lipophilic dye labeling doesn’t affect immunodetection of vesicle surface markers, offering theoretical support for optimized labeling [
251
]. nFCM has also evaluated different EVs isolation methods, finding traditional UC still yields high-purity vesicles in plasma, highlighting its reference value in standardized method development [
252
].
Quantitative polymerase chain reaction (qPCR)
qPCR, monitoring fluorescence signal dynamics, can detect low-abundance nucleic acids in EVs, and each experiment takes only a few hours [
253
]. With high sensitivity and specificity, qPCR is vital for studying vesicle function and disease links [
254
,
255
]. In recent years, researchers have developed various innovative strategies to enhance the efficiency and accuracy of qPCR detection [
256
]. Given the low concentration of nucleic acids in EVs, signal amplification technologies—such as strand displacement reaction (SDR), hybridization chain reaction (HCR), rolling circle amplification (RCA), CRISPR/Cas, and catalytic hairpin assemblies—are often employed to boost detection sensitivity [
190
]. Moreover, RT-qPCR faces the challenge of lacking universal and stably expressed reference genes (RGs) for EVs. To address the standardization issue, recent studies have identified four stably expressed RGs (e.g., SNRPG, OST4). These universal EVs RGs are expected to promote the use of RT-qPCR in analyzing EV-related RNA cargo [
257
].
Total internal reflection fluorescence (TIRF)
TIRF microscopy enables high-sensitivity single-particle dynamic observation and molecular quantification of EVs by exciting fluorescence signals within a nanoscale range of the sample surface [
258
]. The technique allows real-time tracking of the dynamic interactions of 50 nm-sized vesicles. When combined with fluorescent-probe labeling strategies (such as quantum dots or nucleic acid-aptamers), it can also analyze the spatial distribution and content changes of specific molecules within vesicles [
259
].
In recent years, innovative labeling and signal amplification strategies have greatly expanded the application of TIRF technology in detection. For example, using activatable aptamer probes (AAP) specific to tumor markers like PTK7, TIRF can distinguish cancer patients from healthy individuals at the single-vesicle level and monitor treatment effects [
260
]. Researchers have also combined DNA probes’ transient binding characteristics to develop in-situ quantification and stoichiometric methods for miRNA markers in single vesicles [
261
]. In high-throughput detection, a newly-developed high-throughput nano-biochip integrated system (HNCIB) can detect vesicle surface markers and nucleic acids simultaneously [
262
]. Single-vesicle analysis TIRF has made progress by calculating the overlapping proportion of fluorescent signals to distinguish vesicle aggregation and correct quantitative errors [
263
].
Surface-enhanced raman scattering (SERS)
Conventional EVs assays rely on fluorescent or radiolabeling, which can alter surface topology, mask binding sites, or introduce nonspecific signals, thereby obscuring true biological readouts. Label-free detection circumvents these artifacts by directly transducing physicochemical changes triggered by EVs-sensor interactions, enabling unperturbed interrogation of native vesicles [
264
]. Moreover, the exceptional sensitivity of these platforms permits the identification of rare or low-abundance subpopulations, which is pivotal for uncovering disease-relevant EVs-such as those harboring specific oncogenic mutations [
265
].
SERS technology, with its extremely high sensitivity and capability of molecular fingerprint-specific identification, offers a nano-resolution detection solution for analyzing the biochemical composition of EVs [
266
]. Raman techniques for EVs analysis can be divided into labeled and label-free methods. Label-free Raman methods can directly detect and characterize various biomolecular components within EVs, such as proteins, lipids, and nucleic acids, providing information on their composition and potential biological functions. Labeled methods, by using specific tags, reporter molecules, or dyes, can more accurately quantify EVs and selectively detect biomarkers [
267
].
SERS technology has unique photoelectronic properties, is easy to functionalize, and has special interactions between light and nano-metal materials. It can detectosomes ex sensitively without large samples and multiple phenotypic analyses [
268
]. Silver nanoparticle arrays combined with laser processing technologies enable fast label-free recognition of breast cancer exosomes, with single-particle-level sensitivity [
269
]. Integrating SERS aptamer sensors into droplet microfluidic platforms allows high-throughput screening of tumor-related exosomes in blood [
270
]. Detection time is shortened to minutes, making the SERS-based droplet system a potential liquid biopsy technology for early cancer diagnosis [
270
]. Researchers have also developed affinity-based antibody-free enrichment technologies by utilizing the affinity between titanium dioxide and vesicle membrane lipids [
271
]. Sample preparation and detection can be completed within 10 min, with a detection limit as low as hundreds of particles per milliliter [
271
]. Despite these advances, SERS-based EVs detection faces challenges such as insufficient signal stability and non-specific adsorption interference from complex samples like whole blood [
272
]. To address these, researchers have proposed multimodal probe designs, such as combined PTK7 and EpCAM marker detection. Dual-target validation has increased cancer patient identification accuracy to 80% [
273
].
Surface plasmon resonance (SPR)
SPR technology enables label-free and dynamic detection of EVs surface markers by monitoring optical signal changes during molecular binding [
274
]. SPR offers advantages like no labeling, low sample volume (microliters), fast detection (minutes), and high sensitivity for low-concentration targets [
275
]. It can also specifically identify and quantify EVs by combining specific antibodies or aptamers [
276
].
With the continuous breakthroughs in nanomaterial engineering and molecular recognition technologies, SPR is expected to develop towards higher throughput and multi-target simultaneous detection [
277
]. For instance, by optimizing the combination of specific aptamers and G-quadruplex DNA nanostructures, highly sensitive and specific detection of breast cancer-related vesicle markers has been achieved [
278
]. In another study, based on hydrogel-AuNP supramolecular spheres (H-Au), the linear range and detection limit were greatly improved by integrating the signal amplification effect of mass accumulation hydrogels and the LSPR effect of AuNPs with high specificity aptamers [
279
]. Additionally, SPR technology, utilizing aptamer recognition and polydopamine-functionalized gold nanoparticles (Au@PDA NPs) assisted signal amplification for exosome-specific detection, can specifically detect SMMC-7721 exosomes and has the ability to directly detect serum samples [
280
]. The ECL immune sensor based on the LSPR of AuNPs and Pdots can detect pancreatic cancer exosomes and improve the ECL efficiency of Pdots, providing new insights for liquid biopsy technology [
281
]. By optimizing the gold-silica core-shell structure to enhance the SPR effect, ultra-sensitive and selective detection of PD-L1 exosomes in serum has been achieved [
282
].
