Integrating AFM with fluorescence microscopy enables simultaneous measurement of individual vesicle physical properties and surface marker expression. Analyzing EVs from three cell lines revealed multiple subpopulations, confirming vesicle population heterogeneity [
289
]. A new technique called SEVEN, combining affinity isolation and quantitative single-molecule localization microscopy (qSMLM), has quantified the number, size, shape, tetraspanin content, and heterogeneity of affinity-isolated EVs [
284
]. Nano-projectile secondary ion MS (NP-SIMS) with single-particle bombardment analyzes population heterogeneity at single-EV resolution, distinguishing liver cancer-derived EVs from normal ones [
285
].
A droplet microfluidic system with antibody-DNA conjugation detects membrane proteins and mRNA at the single-particle level, enhancing tumor-related vesicle detection sensitivity and specificity [
287
]. nFCM, integrating light scattering and fluorescence detection, analyzes single EV and DNA fragments. It provides physical size and nucleic acid composition information of EVs, offering a high-resolution tool for EV-DNA interaction studies at the single-particle level [
286
]. Polymyxin B-fluorescein probes (PmBF) targeting bacterial lipopolysaccharides combined with nano-flow cytometry to specifically identify and quantify bEVs in blood [
290
]. Single-exosome sequencing based on the 10x Genomics platform deciphers RNA composition and molecular sorting mechanisms of individual EV, applicable to all exosome types [
291
].
High-throughput nano-biochip systems (HNCIB) with integrated protein and nucleic acid detection support parallel analysis of 384 samples in six hours, efficient for large-scale clinical research [
262
]. The single-particle automated Raman trapping analysis system (SPARTA) compares multiple EVs spectra via visualized spectral dimensionality reduction, achieving over 95% sensitivity and specificity in distinguishing cancerous and non-cancerous EVs [
292
]. Digital droplet immune PCR (ddiPCR), combining immune PCR’s specificity and digital droplet PCR’s sensitivity, with machine-learning algorithms enables precise breast-cancer diagnosis via multi-subpopulation EVs counting [
288
]. Two high-sensitivity EV-marker detection methods using single-molecule array technology (SiMoa) analyze tumor-related EVs directly from patient plasma [
293
].
By integrating multi-omics strategies, investigators have systematically assessed EVs heterogeneity. Differential-density-gradient centrifugation first achieves high-purity, reproducible physical separation of large and small vesicles, laying an unbiased foundation for downstream quantification. Subsequent coupling of label-free/targeted proteomics with bulk- or single-EV transcriptomics captures the full molecular spectrum at the population level while resolving subpopulation-specific differences at single-vesicle resolution [
294
]. Complementarily, multivariate analytical pipelines-principally PCA followed by SVM/CNN classifiers and SHAP/PDP explainability modules-have been applied to extract and interpret molecular signatures from EV Raman spectra. These spectral differences mirror functional disparities in tumor-microenvironment remodeling, signal transduction, and immune evasion, thereby tightly linking multivariate outputs to both molecular phenotypes and biological functions of EV subsets [
295
].Furthermore, a method termed “EV Fingerprinting” has been developed that pairs single-particle flow cytometry with high-dimensional data reduction (UMAP and HDBSCAN) to dissect EV heterogeneity at unprecedented resolution. Membrane labeling with the fluorescent dye Di-8-ANEPPS enables simultaneous quantification of EV size, membrane composition, and membrane order through multiparametric analysis [
296
]. A novel liquid-biopsy protocol that employs NanoFisher nanotrap particles for high-yield isolation of plasma EVs followed by machine-learning-assisted metabolomic profiling achieved perfect discrimination between early-stage gastric cancer and non-disease controls, reliable distinction from benign gastric disorders, and an overall three-class accuracy of 92% in 150 samples, offering a non-invasive, highly sensitive, and specific one-stop solution for early gastric-cancer screening [
297
].
Despite advances in single-particle analysis technologies, clinical application bottlenecks remain, such as lack of standardization, high equipment costs, and complex data analysis. Future development of multimodal platforms and automated algorithms is expected to further translate these technologies into point-of-care testing and precision medicine.
Advances and challenges in preclinical translational research
Recent advances in single-particle profiling, molecular phenotyping, and high-resolution isolation now enable the precise identification and exploitation of functionally distinct EVs subsets, catalysing their tailored application in tumour diagnostics, drug delivery, and tissue regeneration. The clinical translation of EVs in theranostics has significantly outpaced early expectations [
298
]. Analysis of public-reporting repositories shows 471 EV-related clinical trials across over 200 diseases, with the highest proportion in diagnosis and companion diagnostics, and cancer being the most common field [
299
]. EVs are increasingly recognized as highly promising liquid-biopsy biomarkers whose analytical sensitivity and specificity continue to rise. Nowhere is this trajectory steeper than in oncology, where EV-based assays have achieved rapid advances in early detection, molecular subtyping, prognostication, and real-time monitoring of therapeutic response, underscoring their vast clinical potential [
300
]. In therapeutic trials, MSC-EVs are predominant and are used in various fields such as respiratory diseases and tissue-injury repair. Currently, over 40 MSC-EVs-based therapies are in clinical trials [
301
,
302
]. Recently, exosomes from human cells/samples and PDEVs have been notably used in EVs clinical trials [
90
]. Each trial type has diverse EVs sources and uses, indicating that the heterogeneity of EVs contributes to their versatile applications [
303
].
Preclinical research on oncology
The emergence of new technologies has not only improved EVs isolation efficiency and detection accuracy but also provided novel strategies and advantages for tumor early diagnosis, gene therapy, and prognostic monitoring. This has greatly enhanced the clinical value of EVS in oncology. With a broad development prospect, they are expected to become a key driver in tumor precision medicine.
Biomarker
Exosomes, a subclass of EVs, have gained significant attention in cancer diagnosis due to their potential for early detection and accurate identification of various cancer types [
304
,
305
]. A clinically applicable high-throughput integrated magneto-electrochemical EVs (HiMEX) technique has been developed. It is capable of analyzing plasma samples within one hour. The results demonstrate that detecting circulating EVs from CRC patients achieves highly accurate cancer diagnosis with an overall accuracy of over 96% [
306
]. The ExoPatch patch enables direct melanoma-specific exosome capture from the skin via a “single-needle” procedure, eliminating the need for separate interstitial fluid extraction. When combined with LFIA for on-site detection, it achieves a sensitivity of 10 EVs/µL for detecting the melanoma-related exosome markers MCAM/MCSP [
307
]. The ColonTrack model demonstrates outstanding clinical diagnostic performance in multicenter validation cohorts, achieving over 95% sensitivity in detecting early-stage colorectal cancer. It represents a significant improvement in diagnostic efficiency compared to traditional markers [
308
]. A detection system combining TiO₂ chemical affinity capture with label-free MS has been established. From large-scale plasma samples, this system has identified GDF15 as a promising EVs protein marker for prostate cancer [
309
].The salivary EVP-based 6-miRNA molecular marker system offers a novel non-invasive strategy for esophageal cancer early detection and risk stratification [
310
]. CAR
+
EVs serve as markers to predict the risk of immune effector cell-associated neurotoxicity syndrome in B-cell lymphoma patients undergoing CD19-targeted CAR T-cell therapy [
311
]. Most EV-DNA research focuses on cancer, with many reports highlighting its potential as a biomarker. For instance, mutated DNA in small EVs from plasma, such as EGFR T790M, KRAS G12D, and TP53 R273H, are considered potential biomarkers for colon, lung, and pancreatic cancers, respectively [
312
]. A recent study systematically profiled the lectin-affinity glycosylation patterns (LAGPs) of plasma-derived EVs and revealed marked heterogeneity in EV glycosylation during gastric cancer initiation and progression. The authors developed an integrated, one-stop liquid-biopsy platform that combines a lectin microarray with machine-learning algorithms; in a cohort of 84 samples, the platform achieved 100% discrimination among early-stage cancer, advanced cancer, benign lesions, and healthy controls [
313
]. This non-invasive, high-efficiency tool offers a unified clinical solution for gastric cancer screening, prognostication, and prediction of immunotherapy response. The continuous advancement of EVs isolation and detection techniques has strongly propelled the clinical translation of EVs as tumor biomarkers
(
Fig.
8
)
.
Moreover, EVs heterogeneity markedly compromises biomarker accuracy and predictability. Sub-populations differing in cellular origin or molecular phenotype can convey diametrically opposed prognostic information. As a case in point, PD-1/CD80
+
small extracellular vesicles derived from immune cells (I-sEVs) and PD-L1
+
sEVs released by tumour cells exert opposing functional and prognostic effects during immunotherapy: PD-1/CD80
+
I-sEVs are associated with therapeutic resistance and a “cold” tumour microenvironment, whereas PD-L1
+
sEVs facilitate immune evasion [
314
]. Measuring circulating sEV levels of PD-L1, PD-1 or CD80 individually yields insufficient sensitivity and specificity for predicting immunotherapeutic responses. Consequently, relying solely on bulk EVs counts or a single marker is inadequate to mirror disease status or treatment efficacy. Future investigations should prioritise the isolation and functional characterisation of specific EVs subpopulations to enhance diagnostic precision and prognostic assessment.
