Drug delivery platform
By leveraging the inherent heterogeneity of EVs—tissue tropism and low immunogenicity—and integrating engineering strategies to enhance their targeting specificity and drug-loading capacity, precise drug delivery within the tumor microenvironment can be achieved. Exosomes have shown great potential as therapeutic delivery vehicles in preclinical and clinical studies [
315
]. With natural targeting and protective transport advantages, they’ve successfully delivered chemotherapeutic and nucleic acid drugs precisely [
316
]. The exosome drug exoIL-12, developed through the engEx™ platform, precisely delivers IL-12 to the tumor microenvironment. It has demonstrated good safety and tolerability in clinical trials and is one of the few EVs therapies to reach this stage [
317
]. A new dual-targeted EVs system (dtEVs) has been developed, which, through the dual targeting mechanism of CD64 protein and CK tissue-homing peptides, enables precise delivery of mRNA, siRNA, and chemotherapeutic drugs [
318
]. Exosomes also show great potential in brain cancer treatment due to their ability to cross the blood-brain barrier. For example, exosomes loaded with paclitaxel can reduce cardiotoxicity. Engineered exosomes carrying siRNA or immunomodulatory molecules like TRAIL have significantly inhibited glioblastoma in preclinical models [
319
].
Gene therapy
In the translational application of gene therapy, clinical research now aims to move exosome-based gene therapy into human trials, bridging the gap from preclinical studies to clinical use [
320
]. Researchers are developing a DTMI therapy that uses tumor-cell-derived drug-loaded EVs to activate macrophages and neutrophils, effectively managing malignant pleural and peritoneal effusions. Approved for clinical use in multiple regions, DTMI therapy, when combined with PD-1 antibodies, can expand the population eligible for treatment [
321
]. For non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC) with bone metastasis and immune resistance, the TMTP1-TSRP-EVs system has been devised, it significantly suppresses tumor growth and bone metastasis in mouse models and reverses PD-L1-mediated resistance [
322
]. Engineered EVs targeting cancer-associated fibroblasts (CAFs), such as IEVs-PFD/138, have also made progress. They can reprogram the pancreatic cancer TME via regulatory molecule delivery, boosting chemotherapeutic efficacy and extending patient survival [
323
]. Moreover, researchers found that EVs from solid tumors, carrying PD-L1 and tumor antigens, can promote treatment resistance by inhibiting CAR-T cell function. But this can be mitigated either by suppressing EVs secretion or by modifying CAR-T cells, like developing Serpin B9-enhanced CAR-T cells, thus improving solid tumor treatment outcomes [
324
].
Prognostic monitoring
In exosome-based prognostic monitoring studies, researchers used TCGA data to create the EALncRI model, which includes MIR4713HG and ZEB1-AS1. This model effectively differentiates patients’ survival risks and predicts immunotherapy responses and chemosensitivity [
325
]. A multicenter meta-analysis covering 56 clinical trials (5,985 patients) showed that single biomarkers, molecular combinations, and vesicle counts in blood EVs are significantly associated with colorectal cancer patients’ survival (pHR = 1.87–3.97), especially in advanced cases [
326
]. By analyzing miRNAs in serum - derived EVs, a diagnostic and prognostic model (sEVmiR-EOC) based on 7 miRNAs was developed. It demonstrated high diagnostic efficiency (AUC of 0.913–0.973) across training, testing, and validation sets and effectively predicted patients’ progression-free survival (PFS) [
327
]. Through proteomics analysis of peritoneal EVs, researchers established molecular profiles related to tumor microenvironment evolution, identified tumor-promoting proteins and pathways involved in cellular communication within the tumor microenvironment, and provided a basis for personalized treatment [
328
].
Preclinical research on tissue repair
The advancement of EVs isolation and detection technologies has also greatly boosted the clinical translation of EVs in tissue repair. These new technologies have increased the purity and efficiency of EVs separation, ensuring the activity of EVs components for tissue regeneration. They also support precise EVs separation from diverse sources, thus speeding up the development of EV-based therapies for damaged tissues and organs
