Nervous system
The research team has developed engineered EVs (EEVs) for intranasal delivery. By integrating ultra-small superparamagnetic nanoparticles (USPN), they have enhanced targeting. These EEVs also carry key miRNAs to inhibit the NF-κB pathway. In animal models and human brain organoids, EEVs have been verified to promote nervous function recovery [
329
]. EV-GFAP in EVs has been identified as a circulating biomarker for TBI severity, guiding early intervention through dynamic monitoring [
333
]. Some researchers have proposed a “pre-depletion-escape” sequential dosing strategy. Using dendritic cell-derived vesicles (DV) combined with CD47-modified vesicles (MV47), this strategy achieves long-term circulation of EVs, significantly improving vascular regeneration and functional recovery after myocardial infarction [
334
].
Skin and wound
A systematic review of 83 preclinical studies indicated that MSC-EVs are markedly superior to the control group in wound closure rate, scar reduction, and angiogenesis (SMD = 1.87–3.97), with particularly significant effects in diabetic wounds [
335
]. A research team discovered that EVs from Streptococcus mutans (Sm EVs) can activate the TLR3 pathway via tRNA variants (e.g., tRNA-Met), promoting epithelial cell proliferation and migration, and significantly accelerating skin wound healing. This mechanism has been verified in human oral mucosa organoids, offering new insights for chronic wound treatment [
336
]. Another team developed a novel synthetic exosome-like vesicle (LPEx) by hybridizing watermelon-derived EVs (wEV) with liposomes and encapsulating 28 key miRNAs. Results show LPEx can significantly accelerate wound healing and inhibit scar formation in a deep second-degree burn model [
330
]. A study on an antibacterial hydrogel (Gel/PL-5) loaded with engineered EVs targeting vascular endothelial cells (s-EV@Gel/PL-5) found that modifying the EVs surface to express sLeX ligands enhances targeting of E-selectin overexpressed in diabetic wounds, significantly accelerating diabetic infected wound healing. The hydrogel also has a sustained-release function to prolong the therapeutic effect [
337
].
Respiratory system
Researchers used allogeneic adipose-derived mesenchymal stem cell-derived EVs (ADSC-EVs) for aerosol inhalation therapy for ARDS. Clinical trials (
NCT04313647
) showed healthy volunteers tolerated aerosolized EVs well with no severe adverse reactions. In animal models, EVs significantly reduced pulmonary inflammatory factor levels and improved survival rates [
338
]. Another team engineered EVs to efficiently load Wnt3a protein (Wnt3aWGEVs) to activate the Wnt/β-catenin signaling pathway. Results showed intravenously administered Wnt3aWGEVs significantly improved lung function and alveolar structural abnormalities, offering a new COPD treatment strategy [
331
]. It was found that ADSC-EVs deliver miR-320a to inhibit the CBL/AMPK pathway, improving mitochondrial energy metabolism and reducing cell apoptosis. They significantly alleviated lung injury in a Staphylococcus aureus-induced ALI model [
339
]. Moreover, studies found that exosomes from hucMSCs cultured in 3D microspheres-have stronger anti-inflammatory and tissue-repair abilities than traditionally-cultured exosomes. By modulating macrophage energy metabolism, they significantly improved LPS-induced ALI models [
340
].
Musculoskeletal system
Researchers have developed a composite system (Rh Gel@SP-EVs). It can regulate energy metabolism, such as promoting ATP synthesis, and inhibit the JAK/STAT pathway. This helps reduce cartilage damage, promotes joint function recovery, and significantly slows the progression of osteoarthritis [
332
]. Another team found that flat matrix (fEVs) can promote the proliferation of aged MPCs, while nano-topographic matrix (nEVs) can enhance myogenic differentiation. By optimizing the delivery sequence, they successfully restored muscle fiber size and contractile function in aged mouse models to levels similar to those in young mice [
341
]. In addition, researchers used EVs derived from hypoxic bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells (Hypo-EVs) combined with an injectable polymeric hydrogel. This approach significantly promoted bone regeneration in cranial defect areas. The hydrogel’s sustained-release system prolonged the local action time of EVs, thereby enhancing the therapeutic effect [
342
].
Other preclinical studies
Most published clinical studies on MSCs and their EVs have used intravenous administration. These studies have confirmed the safety of MSCs and MSC-EVs treatments, but their efficacy may be suboptimal, possibly due to the administration route. In contrast, nebulized inhalation has unique advantages in lung injury treatment, as it significantly increases drug bioavailability in target pulmonary areas [
338
]. In-depth analysis of several clinical trials revealed that in a preliminary trial, MSC-EVs nebulization accelerated pulmonary lesion absorption in COVID-19 pneumonia patients and shortened hospital stays, with no acute or secondary allergic reactions [
343
]. These findings have led researchers to investigate the advantages of local EVs administration. Compared to traditional intravenous injection, nebulization improves drug bioavailability in the lungs, potentially overcoming the efficacy limitations of systemic administration.
Studies have revealed the complex mechanisms of MSC-EVs in treating various diseases, which are hard to fully explain due to disease-specific differences. But by identifying and quantifying EVs attributes tied to expected bioactivity in EV-mediated therapy, we may develop robust potency assays [
344
]. Chromatography advances have enabled efficient clinical-grade pEVs purification. First-in-human trials confirmed that locally injected LEAP-pEVs heal wounds safely and tolerably [
345
]. ExoFlo, an MSC-EVs product, showed good safety and potential efficacy in treating COVID-19-related ARDS patients, with its high purity lowering allergy risks [
346
]. In a double-blind controlled trial on severe COVID-19-related ARDS patients, hPMSC-EVs significantly cut mortality [
347
]. In metabolic regulation, research found that dietary n-3 PUFAs alter EVs’ quantity, composition, and function. By changing their fatty acid and protein profiles, EVs supporting thrombin generation are highly significant for thrombosis prevention and treatment [
348
]. Lastly, a study on complex perianal fistulas showed that local placental MSC-EVs injection enables long - term lesion repair, with treatment effects verified by MRI [
349
].
Future directions and challenges in clinical translation
Clinical translation of EVs is advancing rapidly, with clinical trials targeting a spectrum of diseases already under way (Table
3
). Early-phase studies have established the preliminary safety and efficacy of nebulized MSC-EVs formulations in COVID-19–associated acute respiratory distress syndrome and other pulmonary disorders [
346
]. Engineered EVs platforms are being driven forward through ongoing clinical investigations, underscoring their potential for precise molecular intervention. Meanwhile, EV-based diagnostics continue to demonstrate exceptional accuracy in non-invasive cancer detection. Future advances will hinge on the integrated deployment of cutting-edge technologies. Single-vesicle profiling and multi-omics analyses will dissect extracellular vesicle heterogeneity and functional attributes [
350
], while artificial intelligence and machine-learning algorithms will enable the discovery of disease-specific biomarkers and the optimization of isolation protocols. Research priorities should also encompass engineering EVs for targeted delivery and implementing hybrid techniques such as TFF-SEC to achieve scalable, GMP-compliant production [
351
].
