EVs hold immense translational promise, yet their clinical implementation remains hindered by the absence of rigorously standardized isolation and characterization protocols [
355
]. Inter-study discrepancies in purity and functional potency compromise reproducibility and reliability. Moreover, an incomplete understanding of in vivo biodistribution and clearance kinetics constrains the design of predictable therapeutic strategies [
356
]. In summary, while EVs stand at the forefront of biomarker discovery and regenerative medicine, realizing their pre-clinical potential as clinical reality demands cross-disciplinary collaboration to establish standardized practices, validate clinical utility, and ultimately unlock the transformative capacity of extracellular vesicles in modern medicine.
Perspectives and challenges
As key cell-to-cell communication mediators, EVs have seen research-tech breakthroughs. Innovations span efficient isolation (AF4, microfluidic chips), precise identification (single-particle MS, SERs), and functional analysis (single-exosome sequencing, TIRF). These multi-dimensional advances have deepened our understanding of EVs, unraveling their roles in tumor metastasis, immune regulation, and tissue repair. They’ve also spurred EVs applications in liquid biopsies and drug delivery [
24
]. The central idea driving EVs technology is a strategic pivot from “separation” to “resolution”. Emerging tools now offer tailor-made strategies to dissect EV heterogeneity, with the choice of technique dictated by which dimension—size, origin, cargo, or function—is most pertinent to the biological or clinical question at hand. Only by resolving, rather than merely isolating, heterogeneity can we unlock the full precision of EVs for diagnosis and therapy.
Consensus definitions for EVs subtypes are urgently required. These should extend beyond particle size and biogenesis to incorporate molecular cargo, cellular origin, and functional properties. Multi-parametric frameworks that integrate physical (size, density), biochemical (protein, lipid, and nucleic-acid profiles), and functional (targeting and uptake efficiency) attributes are needed to classify EVs into biologically and clinically relevant subpopulations. A standardized EVs-typing scheme will facilitate cross-study comparisons and enhance reproducibility.
The clinical translation of EVs faces several major challenges. The lack of technical standardization is a key issue; for example, different isolation methods can result in product heterogeneity [
194
]. In addition, there are bottlenecks in scaled production, which is currently low in yield and high in cost [
357
]. EVs long-term therapeutic effects verification is inadequate, and the safety and persistence of repeated dosing are not clear [
358
]. Regardless of the clinical indication, exosome use must comply with Good Manufacturing Practice (GMP). GMP-compliant exosome production involves top-quality materials, cells, and culture conditions, as well as advanced manufacturing techniques and skilled workers, all within strictly controlled and monitored settings. Purification and quality control analyses are also essential to ensure exosomes meet the highest standards before patient use [
359
].
To fully realize their potential, future investigations must adopt a multivariate analytical framework that simultaneously interrogates EVs size, composition, cellular origin, and functional attributes. Population-level analyses yield averaged metrics that are indispensable for biomarker discovery and scalable production, whereas single-vesicle technologies expose the diversity and rarity of specific subpopulations. Integrating these complementary data streams through computational modelling and machine learning will bridge the gap between aggregate trends and individual EV features, enabling more precise linkage of EVs heterogeneity to clinical phenotypes.While initial studies have shown the potential of EVs as drug delivery systems and therapeutic agents, their clinical application needs strict preclinical and clinical trials to verify safety and efficacy [
360
]. A report summarizing 21 human studies and clinical trials using cell-derived EVs therapy for various conditions and diseases has been used for safety meta-analysis and evaluation of effectiveness outcomes. The results indicate that EVs-based therapies are safe, with low rates of serious adverse and adverse events, suggesting they could be promising for treatment [
358
]. Also, selecting proper loading methods, improving the loading efficiency of drugs or bioactive molecules in EVs, and optimizing delivery routes and targeting have become key research focuses. Advances in EVs engineering offer new opportunities to enhance EVs’ therapeutic effects [
361
]. Technologies gene editing and synthetic biology can effectively modify EVs to improve their efficacy and targeting.
EVs-based therapy has shown disease-treatment benefits in preclinical studies, but its long-term efficacy and persistence are still uncertain [
362
]. In-depth research is crucial to clarify EVs’ in-vivo lifespan, drug-efficacy duration, and the safety and effectiveness of repeated dosing [
363
]. Relevant studies indicate that storage at −80 °C and freeze-thaw cycles may significantly alter EVs samples, altering their functional properties. Experimentally, numerous particles undergoing fusion suggest the samples have experienced intense re-modeling and generated multiple components [
97
]. Given this, appropriate storage and transportation conditions for EVs products are an urgent issue to resolve. Recent research on storage conditions and bioactivity of natural EVs and cargo-loaded EVs shows that lyophilization (freeze-drying) followed by room-temperature storage can maintain high bioactivity in cargo-loaded EVs [
364
]. These findings highlight the need for additional measures to ensure EVs stability and effectiveness when validating their feasibility as clinical products [
365
].
The future development of EVs encompasses several key areas: First, developing precise diagnostic markers to enhance disease detection accuracy. Second, delving deeper into EVs’ therapeutic mechanisms across different diseases. Third, optimizing targeted delivery systems to boost therapeutic effects and reduce side effects. Fourth, rigorously evaluating long-term safety and efficacy to ensure clinical reliability. Fifth, advancing translational research to fast-track laboratory findings into clinical use. Sixth, enforcing strict regulatory approval to guarantee product quality and safety. Addressing these challenges requires ongoing research investment and innovation. Interdisciplinary collaboration is indispensable in driving EVs research, involving biology, medicine, chemistry, physics, and other fields [
366
]. It necessitates coordinated efforts from experts in different disciplines to integrate diverse theories and technologies, and to jointly tackle EV-related challenges in disease diagnosis, therapy, and translation [
42
]. While EVs show great biomedical potential, their separation, detection, and standardized preparation face challenges like heterogeneous separation technologies and detection limitations [
367
]. Future research should focus on technological innovation and quality control. Developing more efficient separation techniques, precise detection methods, and stringent standardization processes will propel EVs’ clinical and industrial applications, enabling their widespread use in disease diagnosis and treatment.
